In this lesson, we'll look at how to differentiate a polynomial by applying one simple rule to each term. The big idea is that you don't need to do anything tricky like expanding or simplifying first. You just go term by term, and the workhorse is the power rule, which says the derivative of x to the n is n times x to the n minus one. You bring the exponent down in front, then reduce the exponent by one. So x cubed becomes three x squared, and x to the fifth becomes five x to the fourth, and so on. There are a few companion rules you'll lean on too. The derivative of any constant on its own is zero. If a term is just a number times a function, you keep the number and differentiate the rest. And finally, the derivative of a sum or a difference is just the sum or the difference of the derivatives, so you can handle each piece separately. A neat side effect is that the degree of the polynomial always drops by one. A degree five polynomial gives a degree four derivative, a degree four gives a degree three, and so on. Now let's see it in action.